WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.480
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.936
A bridge is a clever way to measure resistance by finding a balance rather

00:00:05.936 --> 00:00:06.140
than

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:07.640
measuring a large current accurately.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.940
Let's label the circuit clearly.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.560
The source connects between left node A and right node D.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.840
The upper path contains resistor P from A to B, then Q from B to D.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.600
The lower path contains R from A to C, then S from C to D.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:26.980
A galvanometer connects the two midpoints B and C.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.220
If their potentials are different, current can flow through that detector.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.680
At balance, their potentials are equal, so detector current is zero.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.520
This does not mean current is zero everywhere.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.040
Both outer paths can still carry current and dissipate power.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.180
Think of two voltage dividers whose middle points reach the same potential.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.740
Only then can the detector branch be omitted while calculating the outer path

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:50.080
currents.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.580
Before balance is established, removing it generally changes the circuit.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.800
Our model uses positive ohmic resistances, ideal connecting wires and a

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.600
sensitive galvanometer with finite resistance.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.840
The source voltage is nonzero.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.580
A broken detector circuit could also show zero current, but that is not

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.440
evidence of balance.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.240
In a question or an experiment, identify the source diagonal and the detector

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.580
diagonal, then confirm which pair of nodes has equal potential.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.160
That node reasoning is more reliable than memorizing the shape of a diamond.

00:01:23.016 --> 00:01:26.016
Let's derive the balance equation from the labeled circuit.

00:01:26.776 --> 00:01:31.736
At a genuine null, the upper path carries one current through P and Q, and the

00:01:31.736 --> 00:01:33.976
lower path carries another through R and S.

00:01:34.876 --> 00:01:39.436
Equal midpoint potentials mean the drop from A to B equals the drop from A to

00:01:39.436 --> 00:01:39.696
C.

00:01:40.416 --> 00:01:43.996
Therefore upper current times P equals lower current times R.

00:01:44.896 --> 00:01:49.196
Likewise, the drops from B to D and C to D are equal, giving upper current

00:01:49.196 --> 00:01:51.896
times Q equal to lower current times S.

00:01:51.896 --> 00:01:54.416
Divide the first equation by the second.

00:01:55.036 --> 00:01:58.576
The currents cancel, leaving P over Q equal to R over S.

00:01:59.316 --> 00:02:01.936
Equivalently, P times S equals Q times R.

00:02:02.476 --> 00:02:06.136
The cancellation explains why source voltage and detector resistance do not

00:02:06.136 --> 00:02:07.776
appear in the ideal balance ratio.

00:02:08.716 --> 00:02:13.456
Let's use P equal to two ohms, Q equal to three ohms and R equal to four ohms.

00:02:14.316 --> 00:02:16.136
Balance requires S equal to six ohms.

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:20.747
Across a ten volt source, the upper path carries two amperes and the lower

00:02:20.747 --> 00:02:20.996
path

00:02:20.996 --> 00:02:21.876
one ampere.

00:02:22.196 --> 00:02:24.316
Their A to midpoint drops are both four volts.

00:02:25.136 --> 00:02:29.936
Taking D as zero potential, B and C are both at six volts, so the detector has

00:02:29.936 --> 00:02:31.036
no potential difference.

00:02:31.636 --> 00:02:35.402
The equivalent resistance is five ohms in parallel with ten ohms, or ten

00:02:35.402 --> 00:02:35.716
thirds

00:02:35.716 --> 00:02:36.296
of an ohm.

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:38.676
Total source current is three amperes.

00:02:39.236 --> 00:02:43.164
Check the arm labels before writing any ratio; another drawing may use the

00:02:43.164 --> 00:02:43.376
same

00:02:43.376 --> 00:02:44.556
letters in different positions.

00:02:44.784 --> 00:02:49.584
A metre bridge replaces one resistor pair with two parts of the same uniform

00:02:49.584 --> 00:02:50.404
resistance wire.

00:02:51.464 --> 00:02:55.984
Put unknown resistance X in the left upper gap and known resistance S in the

00:02:55.984 --> 00:02:56.644
right upper gap.

00:02:57.264 --> 00:03:01.104
Their common junction connects through the galvanometer to a sliding contact,

00:03:01.104 --> 00:03:03.064
called the jockey, on the lower wire.

00:03:03.924 --> 00:03:05.744
The source connects across the two ends.

00:03:06.624 --> 00:03:10.884
Let the full wire length be L, and let the jockey be distance l from the left

00:03:10.884 --> 00:03:12.604
end, the side containing X.

00:03:12.604 --> 00:03:17.404
At null, the Wheatstone ratio gives X over S equal to the resistance of the

00:03:17.404 --> 00:03:19.624
left wire part divided by that of the right part.

00:03:20.424 --> 00:03:24.484
For a uniform wire at a uniform temperature, resistance equals resistivity

00:03:24.484 --> 00:03:26.084
times length divided by area.

00:03:26.904 --> 00:03:31.184
Resistivity and cross sectional area cancel in the ratio, leaving l divided by

00:03:31.184 --> 00:03:31.984
L minus l.

00:03:32.904 --> 00:03:36.354
The familiar one hundred minus l appears only when the total length is one

00:03:36.354 --> 00:03:38.864
hundred centimeters and l uses centimeters too.

00:03:39.504 --> 00:03:43.404
Now take a three ohm known resistance and a null at forty centimeters.

00:03:44.244 --> 00:03:47.984
The unknown is three times forty divided by sixty, giving two ohms.

00:03:48.804 --> 00:03:50.344
Why must it be smaller than the standard?

00:03:50.984 --> 00:03:53.204
Because its corresponding wire segment is shorter.

00:03:53.784 --> 00:03:55.164
This is a quick physical check.

00:03:55.764 --> 00:03:58.604
If you measure l from the other end, the ratio reverses.

00:03:59.424 --> 00:04:02.284
Draw the gap labels and state the measurement end before calculating.

00:04:02.764 --> 00:04:05.564
The uniform wire assumption is doing real work here.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.180
Let's make the previous example do more work.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:13.440
Unknown X is two ohms, standard S is three ohms, and the original balance is

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.120
forty centimeters from the left end.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.740
Interchange the gap resistances while keeping the same uniform wire and

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.200
measuring from the same left end.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:26.420
The new ratio is three over two, so the left segment is sixty centimeters and

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:27.560
the right forty.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:30.600
The ideal balance length becomes L minus the original length.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:35.540
Real end resistances can prevent exact complementarity; that mismatch is one

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:36.720
clue to systematic error.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:41.185
Now restore X on the left and connect another two ohm resistor in parallel

00:04:41.185 --> 00:04:41.380
with

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:41.560
it.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.860
The left gap's equivalent resistance is one ohm, while the right gap stays

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:46.520
three ohms.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.820
At balance, l over one hundred minus l equals one third.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:55.220
Multiply through: three l equals one hundred minus l, giving l equal to twenty

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:55.880
five centimeters.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.280
Pause and check the direction of the shift.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:02.680
The left gap resistance decreased, so its corresponding wire segment must

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:03.260
become shorter.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:07.420
The wire did not physically shrink; only the jockey position changed.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:12.100
If instead a resistor is added in series in the gap, add the resistances first

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.700
and then solve the new ratio.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:17.820
Away from balance, the detector can carry current, and you need full circuit

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.260
analysis to find its magnitude.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:22.296
The simple ratio determines a null position, not every detector reading.

00:05:22.296 --> 00:05:25.036
Let's finish by asking what makes a measurement trustworthy.

00:05:25.816 --> 00:05:30.456
A balance very close to one end uses a very short wire segment, so a small

00:05:30.456 --> 00:05:32.816
position error becomes a large fractional error.

00:05:33.696 --> 00:05:37.276
Choose a known resistance close to the unknown so the null lies near the

00:05:37.276 --> 00:05:37.656
midpoint.

00:05:38.336 --> 00:05:42.336
For small uncertainty in l, treating the standard and total length as exact,

00:05:42.896 --> 00:05:46.401
the fractional uncertainty in X is approximately L times the length

00:05:46.401 --> 00:05:46.976
uncertainty

00:05:47.596 --> 00:05:49.696
divided by l times L minus l.

00:05:49.916 --> 00:05:54.016
At forty centimeters on a one hundred centimeter wire, a point one centimeter

00:05:54.016 --> 00:05:58.156
reading uncertainty contributes about zero point four two percent.

00:05:58.156 --> 00:06:02.916
Near five centimeters, the same uncertainty contributes about two point one

00:06:02.916 --> 00:06:03.176
percent.

00:06:03.176 --> 00:06:07.276
This comparison explains the midpoint preference; it is not just a laboratory

00:06:07.276 --> 00:06:07.796
tradition.

00:06:08.536 --> 00:06:11.636
Real measurements also have uncertainty in the standard and total length.

00:06:12.516 --> 00:06:16.936
Wire nonuniformity, end resistance and contact resistance can bias the ideal

00:06:16.936 --> 00:06:17.256
formula.

00:06:18.136 --> 00:06:22.521
Gentle jockey contact and brief current flow help avoid damaging or heating

00:06:22.521 --> 00:06:22.696
the

00:06:22.696 --> 00:06:22.976
wire.

00:06:23.556 --> 00:06:27.516
Interchanging gaps can help assess end effects, but do not claim that every

00:06:27.516 --> 00:06:30.336
systematic error is automatically removed by one swap.

00:06:31.276 --> 00:06:36.596
For JEE, check five things: a functioning detector, a nonzero source, the gap

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:39.809
labels, the end from which length is measured, and the uniform wire

00:06:39.809 --> 00:06:40.336
assumption.

00:06:40.836 --> 00:06:44.896
For a balanced Wheatstone bridge, confirm equal midpoint potentials before

00:06:44.896 --> 00:06:45.696
omitting the detector.

00:06:46.236 --> 00:06:49.910
For a metre bridge, compare the resistance ratio with the segment length

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:50.216
ratio.

00:06:51.036 --> 00:06:52.996
Those two checks bring the entire lesson together.
